<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Secret History of the Mongols</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Secret History of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> is the oldest surviving work of Mongolian literature, written in the 13th century, probably soon after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>. It tells the story of his ancestors, his youth as Temüjin, his struggles with rival steppe peoples such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> and Merkits, and his rise to power. It survives mainly in a Chinese transcription.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/secret-history-of-the-mongols.jpg" alt="Secret History of the Mongols" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Цэндийн Дамдинсүрэн · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SecretHistoryMongols1947.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Secret History of the Mongols</b>, written in 1240, names Tengri many times.</blockquote><a href="/religion/tengrism">Tengrism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 13th to the 15th centuries Mongolian was written in four scripts: the Uyghur Mongolian script, the ’Phags-pa script used in decrees, Chinese characters, in which The <b>Secret History of the Mongols</b> was recorded, and the Arabic script of dictionaries.</blockquote><a href="/language/mongolian">Mongolian · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Genghis Khan promoted men for loyalty and ability rather than birth: Jebe, who according to the <b>Secret History of the Mongols</b> had once shot the khan’s horse from under him in battle, became one of his leading generals.</blockquote><a href="/government/nomadic-confederation">Nomadic confederation · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the <b>Secret History of the Mongols</b>, Temüjin's father Yesügei had stolen his wife Hö'elün from a Merkit, and in revenge the Merkits abducted Temüjin's bride Börte, who was rescued with help from Toghrul of the Keraite Khanate and Jamukha.</blockquote><a href="/state/merkit-confederation">Merkit confederation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Jin dynasty (1115-1234) used them as allies against other steppe peoples; according to the <b>Secret History of the Mongols</b> they handed the Mongol leader Ambaghai to the Jin for execution and poisoned Temüjin's father Yesügei.</blockquote><a href="/state/tatar-confederation">Tatar confederation</a></li></ul></section>
